(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97, the shared-library half. A `.lib` is a signature,
not a body: nothing on the consumer's side can prove what an imported
function writes into a list it is handed, so a list passed to an imported
function is widened conservatively and every read of it dispatches on the
slot's own runtime tag - which `_list_append` stores whichever side of the
boundary the append was written on. That costs the homogeneous fast path
across an import and is right whatever the library does.

The `.lib` cannot answer the question instead. It records an element type,
not a write record, and the three functions here are why:

  'note whatever'    appends a text          -> renders `list of text`
  'report the size'  only reads the length   -> renders bare `list`
  'stash a local'    appends a text it took
                     out of its own list     -> renders bare `list`

'stash a local' widens the caller's list and says nothing about it, so a
consumer that trusted the `.lib` would leave that list on the fast path and
print the appended text's address. And 'note whatever' promises `list of
text` to every caller, including one whose list holds numbers - a consumer
that trusted it would read an integer as a text pointer and segfault. The
conservative widening is the answer to both.

Built with `vox noting_lib.vox --shared -o libnoting.so`, which writes
`noting.lib` beside it; the consumer `see`s the `.lib`.)

Library noting version "1.0".

To 'note whatever' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  append label to noted.

To 'report the size' with a list called noted.
  Print "size: {noted's length}".

To 'stash a local' with a list called noted.
  a list called borrowed is ["borrowed"].
  append element 1 of borrowed to noted.
